<div id=3D"b22"><b1><br>
<br>
In the spectrum of the Fourier transformation the even and odd part are
separated whereas in the Hartley spectrum they are in one value.
Therefore the values of the Hartley spectrum are a little bigger.<br>
<br>
In the spectrum of a cut sinus like:<br>
<br>
</b1></div>
<div id=3D"f22" align=3D"center">
<img alt=3D"DHT" src=3D"https://www.mosismath.com/DigitalSignal/ImgDataHart=
ley/Image_DHT_Sine.jpg"><br>
</div>
<div id=3D"b23"><b1><br>
<br>
that can be seen even better:<br>
<br>
<br>
For this signal the Hartley spectrum is
<br>
<br>
</b1></div>
<div id=3D"f23" align=3D"center">
<img alt=3D"DHT" src=3D"https://www.mosismath.com/DigitalSignal/ImgDataHart=
ley/Image_DHT_17.jpg"><br>
</div>
<div id=3D"b24"><b1><br>
<br>
And the Fourier spectrum:<br>
<br>
</b1></div>
<div id=3D"f24" align=3D"center">
<img alt=3D"DHT" src=3D"https://www.mosismath.com/DigitalSignal/ImgDataHart=
ley/Image_DHT_18.jpg"><br>
</div>
<div id=3D"b25"><b1><br>
<br>
There is one big difference: The first value of the Hartley spectrum is
doubled as big as the same one of the Fourier spectrum. The reason for
that is that this first value is a special case in the Fourier
transformation and is multiplied by 2 in the inverse transformation <img st=
yle=3D"width: 20px; height: 20px;" alt=3D":-)" src=3D"https://www.mosismath=
.com/Smileywink.gif">
<br>
<br>
<br>
<br>
</b1></div>
